<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neretva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neretva is a river of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> that flows about 225 kilometres from the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a> into the Adriatic in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. In antiquity its valley held the Daorsi and the Roman trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narona">Narona</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it gave its name to the Narentines, Slav seafarers whose principality of Pagania was notorious for piracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neretva.jpg" alt="Neretva" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JERRYE AND ROY KLOTZ MD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NERETVA_RIVER_VALLEY_NEAR_POCITELJ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Neretva, also called the Narenta, is the largest karst river of the Dinaric Alps. Of its 225 kilometres, 208 are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and the last 22 in Dubrovnik-Neretva County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The upper course rises at the foot of Zelengora and Lebršnik and cuts gorges 600 to 800 metres deep. Below Jablanica it enters a third canyon, 800 to 1,200 metres deep, between the mountains of Prenj, Čvrsnica and Čabulja. Mostar is the largest town on its banks. Below the confluence of the Trebižat and Bregava the valley spreads into an alluvial fan of 20,000 hectares, and the river splits into several branches to form a delta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In antiquity the river was known as Narenta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narona">Narona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montlaures">Naro</a>, and finds of Illyrian culture, among them ancient shipwrecks at Hutovo Blato, show daily and religious life along it. At Mogorjelo near Čapljina stood a suburban villa of the 4th century with mills, bakeries, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> refinery and forges, destroyed in the middle of that century during an invasion of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>; a church dedicated to Hermagor was built on its ruins at the end of the 5th.</p><p>In 1557 the Ottoman sultan Suleiman commissioned the Old Bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a> to replace a wooden suspension bridge, designed by Mimar Hayruddin, a student of Sinan. Its inscription dates its completion to 974 AH, between 19 July 1566 and 7 July 1567. Artillery fire destroyed it on 9 November 1993, and it was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> from 1,088 stones and opened on 23 July 2004. In early 1943 some 20,000 Partisans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> were caught with their backs to the river near Jablanica while they struggled to save some 4,500 wounded and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> patients from some 150,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Four large dams on the main stream supply electricity, flood protection and water storage, and the lake at Jablanica, made in 1953, flooded a fertile valley known simply as Neretva since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> land reclamation has reduced the delta's twelve branches to three. The delta has been a Ramsar site since 1992 and Hutovo Blato, now a nature park, since 2001. The basin shelters many endemic fish, including three species of trout.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neretva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Daorsi emerged in the <b>Neretva</b> valley of Herzegovina, in contact with Greek traders at Narona and the island colonies of the Adriatic.</blockquote><a href="/state/daorsi">Daorsi</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 3rd century BCE the Ardiaean dynasty under Agron united the coast from the <b>Neretva</b> to Epirus, with Scodra (Shkodër) and Rhizon (Risan) as royal seats.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Narentines were Slavs who settled the coast between the Cetina and <b>Neretva</b> rivers in the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagania">Pagania</a></li><li><blockquote>Hum emerged among Slavs settled between the <b>Neretva</b> and Dubrovnik in the 7th to 9th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
